"""A Level Religious Studies (0785), Upper Sixth: New Testament: Ethics & Eschatology (3 lessons), Alternative Track: Church History,
Ethics or Islam (5 lessons) and Examination (Paper 1, Paper 2, Paper 3: 3 lessons).

Descriptive and scholarly: 'Christians believe', 'Muslims believe', 'scholars argue'. Scripture is paraphrased; chapters and verses are given only
where certain (translations differ). The Alternative Track topics are options: students should check which track their school teaches. Facts about
the examination come from the Board's 2023 Advanced Level subject report (paraphrased, no past-paper content). Church history in Cameroon gives only
well-established outlines; dates and names are hedged 'check your textbook'. Passages and cases written for practice are invented.
"""

from .dsl import *  # noqa: F403

COURSE = ("religious-studies", "upper-sixth")

M_ETH = "New Testament: Ethics & Eschatology"
M_ALT = "Alternative Track: Church History, Ethics or Islam"
M_EXAM = "Examination"

TRACK = ("**Alternative Track.** This topic belongs to a track that your school may or may not teach. Check which track your school follows before "
         "you spend long on it. The Board's 2023 report shows where such material meets the papers: Philosophy of Religion, Ethics and Morality, "
         "and the Historical Development of Islam are named sections of Paper 3.")

LESSONS = []

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1. Sermon on the Mount
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_ETH, topic="The ethics of the Sermon on the Mount",
    title="The ethics of the Sermon on the Mount",
    slug="rsu2-sermon-on-the-mount",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Matthew 5 to 7 gathers the teaching of Jesus on the ethics of the kingdom. This lesson covers its shape, the Beatitudes, the antitheses, the three acts of piety and the main scholarly ways of reading it.",
    objectives=[
        "Outline the main sections of the Sermon on the Mount and say how it relates to Luke's Sermon on the Plain",
        "Explain the Beatitudes and the six antitheses as an ethic of the heart and not only of rules",
        "Describe four scholarly interpretations of the Sermon (interim ethic, impossible ideal, counsels of perfection, kingdom ethic)",
        "Evaluate how practical the Sermon is for a modern Christian",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Beatitudes", "Antitheses", "Fulfilling the Law", "Interim ethic", "Kingdom ethic", "Righteousness of the heart"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Think of a school assembly where the principal gives rules, and then a teacher says: 'Rules are not enough. I want you to change from inside.' The Sermon on the Mount works a little like that. In Matthew's Gospel, chapters 5 to 7, Jesus teaches his disciples, with crowds listening, what life under God's rule looks like. It is the best-known block of ethical teaching in the New Testament, and it is central to this part of the syllabus."),
        T("The Board's papers ask candidates to relate Gospel teaching to the setting in which it was given, and to avoid pure story-telling. So in this lesson we keep asking two questions: what does the teaching say, and what does it mean for a follower of Jesus?"),
        H("Setting and shape"),
        D("The **Sermon on the Mount** is the first of five great teaching blocks in Matthew's Gospel (Matthew 5 to 7). Matthew places Jesus on a mountain, which many scholars link to Moses and Mount Sinai, so that Jesus appears as a teacher of the Law for the new community. Luke has a shorter parallel, the **Sermon on the Plain** (Luke 6), and scholars debate whether these come from a shared source or from separate occasions. Check your textbook for the view your course follows.", title="Definition"),
        KEY("Outline of Matthew 5 to 7", "Most textbooks divide the Sermon like this. Learn the order so that you can place any verse.", cards=[
            ["5:3-16", "Beatitudes, then the disciples as salt and light."],
            ["5:17-48", "Jesus and the Law: 'I have not come to abolish but to fulfil', then the six antitheses."],
            ["6:1-18", "True piety: giving, prayer (with the Lord's Prayer) and fasting, done for God and not for show."],
            ["6:19-7:12", "Treasure, serving two masters, anxiety, judging others, asking God, and the Golden Rule."],
            ["7:13-27", "Two ways, true and false teachers, the wise and foolish builders."],
        ]),
        H("The Beatitudes"),
        T("A **beatitude** is a statement of blessing: 'Blessed are ...'. The Beatitudes name the people whom God favours: the poor in spirit, those who mourn, the meek, those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, the merciful, the pure in heart, the peacemakers, and those persecuted for righteousness. Each is promised a reward that belongs to the kingdom, such as comfort, mercy or seeing God."),
        T("Scholars notice that the Beatitudes describe a **character** more than a list of jobs. They also turn the usual values upside down: the world admires the strong and the wealthy, but Jesus blesses the humble and the gentle. Luke's version is shorter and speaks more directly of the poor and hungry, and adds woes to the rich, so many readers say that Luke stresses social reversal and Matthew stresses inner attitude. Both emphases appear in the tradition."),
        H("Jesus and the Law"),
        T("Matthew 5:17-20 is the hinge of the Sermon. Jesus says that he has come to **fulfil** the Law and the Prophets, not to abolish them, and that his followers' righteousness must exceed that of the scribes and Pharisees. Scholars read 'fulfil' in different ways: as completing what the Law pointed towards, as bringing out its deepest meaning, or as obeying it perfectly. All of them agree that the Sermon does not throw away the Law, but moves its centre from outward obedience to the intention of the heart."),
        D("The **antitheses** are the six teachings that follow the pattern 'You have heard that it was said ... but I say to you ...' (Matthew 5:21-48). They cover anger and murder, lust and adultery, divorce, oaths, retaliation, and love of enemies.", title="Definition"),
        KEY("What each antithesis does", "Jesus does not replace the old commandment with a weaker one. He goes to the attitude behind the act.", cards=[
            ["Anger", "Not only murder, but the anger and contempt that lead to it."],
            ["Lust", "Not only adultery, but the desire that treats a person as an object."],
            ["Oaths", "Honest speech makes oaths unnecessary: let your yes mean yes."],
            ["Retaliation", "The old law limited revenge to equal harm. Jesus calls for generous, non-violent responses such as turning the other cheek."],
            ["Love of enemies", "The neighbour includes the enemy. God's goodness is the model: his sun rises on all."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: reading an antithesis", intro="Explain what Jesus teaches about anger in Matthew 5:21-26 and how it goes beyond the Law.", steps=[
            S("State the old teaching", text="'You shall not murder' was the commandment. The courts dealt with the act."),
            S("State the new teaching", text="Jesus says that anger, and insulting words, also fall under God's judgement."),
            S("Explain the principle", text="The root of murder is a hostile heart. Jesus moves the focus from the deed to the attitude."),
            S("Give the practical consequence", text="Be reconciled with a brother or sister before offering worship, because broken relationships damage worship."),
            S("Conclusion", text="The antithesis shows an ethic of the heart: obeying the Law means changing the person, not only the behaviour."),
        ]),
        H("True piety and the Lord's Prayer"),
        T("In 6:1-18 Jesus speaks of three acts of religious practice that Jews of his time valued: giving to the poor, prayer and fasting. The warning is the same each time: do not do them to be admired. The reward comes from the Father who sees what is done in secret. The Lord's Prayer sits in the middle. Its parts include honouring God's name, asking for his kingdom and will, asking for daily bread, forgiveness as we forgive others, and rescue from testing."),
        H("Reading the Sermon: scholarly interpretations"),
        T("A fair problem arises: can anyone really live like this? Turning the other cheek, never being angry, giving without worry? Scholars have proposed several answers. Learn each with its critics, because essays ask you to evaluate."),
        KEY("Four main interpretations", "Each tries to explain how an ordinary person can relate to extreme demands.", cards=[
            ["Interim ethic", "Albert Schweitzer argued that Jesus expected the kingdom to come very soon, so the demands were for a short crisis period. Critic: the Sermon has no sign that it expires, and Matthew's church preserved it for lasting use."],
            ["Impossible ideal", "A tradition linked with Lutheran thought says that the demands are meant to show that no one can meet God's standard, so that people turn to grace. Critic: Jesus ends with 'do' the words, as builders on rock."],
            ["Counsels of perfection", "Medieval teaching distinguished commands for all and higher counsels for a few, such as monks. Critic: the Sermon is addressed to disciples in general, and the Reformers rejected two levels of discipleship."],
            ["Kingdom ethic", "Many modern scholars say that the Sermon describes life in the kingdom that has begun, lived in the power of God, not by effort alone. Critic: it can become vague about what exactly to do."],
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Retelling instead of evaluating", "Many candidates list the Beatitudes in order and stop. The Board's report says that answers which only narrate lose marks. State what each teaching means, then link it to the setting, then evaluate."),
        QUIZ("What is the main pattern of the six antitheses?", [
            "They give new laws that replace the Ten Commandments",
            "They take an old commandment and show the attitude of the heart behind it",
            "They tell disciples to avoid the synagogue",
            "They repeat the Law word for word"], 1,
            "Each antithesis says 'You have heard ... but I say', moving from the act to the heart.",
            hint="Think of anger and murder.",
            feedback=["Jesus says he has not come to abolish the Law.", "", "The Sermon does not say this.", "The antitheses go beyond the Law, not repeat it."]),
        QUIZ("Which interpretation says that Jesus expected the end of the world soon, so the Sermon is for a short period?", [
            "Counsels of perfection", "Impossible ideal", "Interim ethic", "Kingdom ethic"], 2,
            "The interim ethic, associated with Schweitzer, ties the demands to an expected short time before the end.",
            hint="The word 'interim' means in between.",
            feedback=["That idea separates levels of discipleship.", "That idea says the demands show human need.", "", "That idea says the kingdom has already begun."]),
        TIP("Exam link. Teaching from the Gospels appears in Paper 3 as structural questions, and in Paper 1 as multiple-choice questions on basic facts. Where a question names a section of the Gospel, always place the teaching in its setting: who is being taught, and why."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("(a) Name three of the Beatitudes. (b) Explain what the Sermon means by fulfilling the Law.",
           "(a) For example: the poor in spirit, those who mourn, the meek, the merciful, the pure in heart, the peacemakers. (b) Jesus says he has not come to abolish the Law and the Prophets but to fulfil them (Matthew 5:17). Scholars read this as completing what the Law pointed to, bringing out its deepest meaning, or obeying it fully. In the antitheses Jesus moves from the outward act to the attitude of the heart, so that righteousness must go beyond that of the scribes and Pharisees."),
        PQ("Explain the teaching of Jesus on retaliation and on loving enemies in Matthew 5:38-48.",
           "Plan: (1) the old rule, an eye for an eye, limited revenge to equal harm; (2) Jesus calls for generous responses such as turning the other cheek and walking the extra mile, which break the cycle of violence; (3) he commands love for enemies and prayer for persecutors; (4) the reason is that God gives sun and rain to all, so disciples copy his goodness; (5) comment: scholars debate whether this forbids all force or teaches personal readiness to forgive, and Christians differ."),
        PQ("'The Sermon on the Mount is too hard to be followed.' Discuss.",
           "Plan: (1) agree: the demands (no anger, love of enemies, no anxiety) seem beyond human strength, and the interim ethic and impossible ideal readings take this seriously; (2) disagree: Jesus ends by telling hearers to do his words, builders on rock, so he expects obedience; (3) kingdom ethic: God's help makes the way of life possible; (4) the Beatitudes show the Sermon describes character growth, not instant perfection; (5) conclusion: a balanced answer says it is demanding, but meant to be lived."),
        SUM("The Sermon on the Mount (Matthew 5 to 7) is the main ethical teaching of Jesus in Matthew; Luke has a shorter Sermon on the Plain.",
            "The Beatitudes name the people God favours and turn worldly values upside down.",
            "The antitheses take an old commandment and move to the attitude of the heart.",
            "True piety is done for God, not to be admired.",
            "Scholars read the Sermon as an interim ethic, an impossible ideal, counsels of perfection or a kingdom ethic; each has critics."),
    ]))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2. State, wealth and marriage
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_ETH, topic="The Christian and the state, wealth and marriage",
    title="The Christian and the state, wealth and marriage",
    slug="rsu2-state-wealth-marriage",
    minutes=25,
    summary="New Testament teaching on three practical areas: how Christians relate to the state (Mark 12, Romans 13, Acts 5, Revelation 13), what they should think of wealth, and what is taught on marriage, divorce and singleness.",
    objectives=[
        "Compare the New Testament texts that urge obedience to the state with those that allow resistance",
        "Explain the teaching on wealth and poverty in the Gospels and the early church",
        "Describe the teaching of Jesus and Paul on marriage, divorce and singleness, with the setting of 1 Corinthians 7",
        "Apply the teachings to a modern case and evaluate them",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Render to Caesar", "Romans 13", "Obey God rather than people", "Treasure in heaven", "Love of money", "Divorce teaching", "Singleness"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Every Christian must answer practical questions. Should I pay tax? What should I do when a law seems wrong? Is it wrong to be rich? What does faithful marriage look like? The New Testament does not give one neat rule book. It gives teaching in particular situations, and scholars must ask how to apply it. This lesson looks at three areas together because each shows how the early church balanced faith and daily life."),
        T("Keep one rule in mind from the Board's report: when a question asks about the setting, show it. The report criticises candidates who write about marriage in general instead of linking Paul's words to the real problems of the Corinthian church."),
        H("The Christian and the state"),
        D("**The state** here means the civil government: in New Testament times, mainly the Roman Empire, its governors, its taxes and its courts.", title="Definition"),
        KEY("Texts that support respect for the state", "These passages ask Christians to be good citizens.", cards=[
            ["Mark 12:13-17", "Asked whether to pay tax to Caesar, Jesus asks for a coin, and says to give Caesar what is Caesar's and God what is God's. Scholars argue about how the two claims relate: some see two separate spheres, others say God's claim is greater."],
            ["Romans 13:1-7", "Paul says that every person should be subject to the governing authorities, since they have a role under God's order, and that Christians should pay taxes and respect. He wrote to Christians in Rome, probably wanting them to live peaceably."],
            ["1 Peter 2:13-17", "Christians should accept human institutions and honour the emperor, yet 'live as free people' who serve God. Good conduct answers false accusation."],
        ]),
        KEY("Texts that set limits", "The same writers also show that the state's claim is not absolute.", cards=[
            ["Acts 5:29", "When the council ordered the apostles to stop preaching, Peter and the apostles answered that they must obey God rather than people."],
            ["Revelation 13", "The beast stands for a power that demands worship that belongs only to God. Many scholars link it to Roman emperor worship, and the book calls Christians to resist."],
            ["Jesus before Pilate", "Jesus accepts that Pilate has authority, but says that it is given from above (John 19:11), which treats earthly power as limited."],
        ]),
        T("So scholars ask how Romans 13 and Revelation 13 fit together. A common answer says that Paul describes the state in its proper role of keeping order and punishing wrongdoing, while Revelation describes a state that has gone wrong. Others say that Paul was only giving advice for a particular crisis. Christians in history have used Romans 13 to teach obedience and Acts 5:29 to defend resistance to unjust rule. In your essay say that readers disagree and give reasons."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a model plan on the state", intro="'The New Testament teaches Christians to obey the state without exception.' Discuss.", steps=[
            S("Opening", text="Introduce the question: the teaching is not one-sided. State that you will present both groups of texts."),
            S("Support", text="Mark 12:17 and Romans 13:1-7 call for tax and respect; Paul sees order as under God."),
            S("Limit", text="Acts 5:29 shows obedience to God overrides orders to stop preaching; Revelation 13 resists claims to worship."),
            S("Evaluation", text="Paul wrote in a certain situation to Rome; he probably did not expect to be used as a defence of any government."),
            S("Conclusion", text="The New Testament teaches general respect and good citizenship, but not obedience without exception."),
        ]),
        H("Wealth and possessions"),
        T("Jesus speaks often about money. In the Sermon on the Mount he says that you cannot serve God and money (Matthew 6:24) and tells disciples to store up treasure in heaven. In the story of the rich man who came to Jesus (Mark 10:17-31), the man keeps the commandments but cannot give up his wealth, and Jesus says how hard it is for the rich to enter the kingdom. In Luke, a parable of a rich fool warns against hoarding, and the story of the rich man and Lazarus (Luke 16:19-31) warns those who ignore the poor at their gate."),
        T("The early church shared goods in Jerusalem (Acts 2 and 4), and Paul organised a collection for the poor Christians in Jerusalem (2 Corinthians 8 and 9). Paul's follower in the letter to Timothy writes that the **love** of money, not money itself, is a root of all kinds of evil (1 Timothy 6:10). Notice the precision: wealth is not called evil, but the love of it and the neglect of others are."),
        KEY("Themes to bring out", "Four ideas appear repeatedly.", cards=[
            ["Danger", "Wealth can make people trust themselves instead of God."],
            ["Generosity", "Disciples share with the needy, as in Jerusalem."],
            ["Justice", "Ignoring poor neighbours is condemned."],
            ["Contentment", "Trust in God replaces anxiety about food and clothes (Matthew 6:25-34)."],
        ]),
        TIP("In a Cameroonian setting you can say that many churches ask members for tithes and offerings and also support schools and clinics. Do not claim figures. Say simply that scholars and church leaders debate whether prosperity should be seen as a sign of God's favour, and that the New Testament texts above give a cautious answer."),
        H("Marriage, divorce and singleness"),
        T("Jesus teaches about marriage when Pharisees test him on divorce (Mark 10:2-12; Matthew 19:3-9). He points back to creation: God made them male and female, the two become one flesh, and what God has joined, people must not separate. He says that Moses allowed divorce because of human hardness of heart. In Mark remarriage after divorce is called adultery. Matthew records an exception clause for sexual unfaithfulness (Matthew 5:32 and 19:9). Scholars disagree about what the exception means and how it should be read. Check your textbook for the view your course follows."),
        T("Paul answers questions that the Corinthians sent him (1 Corinthians 7). Some members were asking whether it was better not to marry at all. Paul says marriage is good and that husband and wife owe each other love and mutual respect, while some, like himself, have a gift to remain single and may serve the Lord with fewer worries. He says that those who are married should not seek divorce, and that if a believer is married to an unbeliever who agrees to stay, the believer should stay; if the unbeliever leaves, the believer is not bound (7:12-16). He also speaks of 'the present distress', which many scholars see as a reason for his preference for staying as one is."),
        T("Ephesians 5:21-33 presents marriage as a relationship of mutual submission and sacrificial love, compared to Christ and the church. Christians read this in different ways, and some debate what it means for the roles of husbands and wives."),
        MISTAKE("General essays on marriage", "Writing about modern marriage problems earns little. Link Jesus' teaching to the Pharisees' question and Paul's teaching to the real Corinthian problems: some members despised marriage, some visited prostitutes, and some asked about mixed marriages."),
        EX("Worked example 2: explain Paul's teaching on marriage in 1 Corinthians 7", intro="Give a structured answer in four points.", steps=[
            S("Setting", text="The Corinthians wrote to Paul with questions. Some ideas about sex and marriage in the city were loose, and some in the church overcorrected by despising marriage."),
            S("Marriage is good", text="Husband and wife owe each other affection and should not deprive each other except by agreement for a short time of prayer."),
            S("Singleness is a gift", text="Paul wishes that all were as he is, but says that each has a gift, one of one kind and one of another."),
            S("Divorce and mixed marriage", text="The married should not separate; a believer should not divorce an unbelieving partner who consents to stay, but is not bound if the partner leaves."),
            S("Comment", text="Paul's reasons include the present distress and his wish that believers be free from worldly anxieties."),
        ]),
        QUIZ("What does 1 Timothy 6:10 say is a root of all kinds of evil?", [
            "Money itself", "The love of money", "Being poor", "Paying tax"], 1,
            "The text says the love of money, which is a greedy attitude, not money itself.",
            hint="Look for the word 'love'.",
            feedback=["The verse does not say money is evil in itself.", "", "Poverty is not called a root of evil.", "Tax is not mentioned in the verse."]),
        QUIZ("Which text shows that apostles put God's command before the order of the authorities?", [
            "Romans 13:1", "Acts 5:29", "1 Peter 2:13", "Mark 12:17"], 1,
            "In Acts 5:29 Peter and the apostles say that they must obey God rather than people.",
            hint="The council told them to stop preaching.",
            feedback=["Romans 13 stresses subjection to the authorities.", "", "1 Peter urges respect for institutions.", "Mark 12:17 is about tax to Caesar."]),
        TIP("Exam link. Ethics from the New Testament can appear as a structural question in Paper 3 (for example on Paul's marriage teaching in 1 Corinthians) or as a Paper 1 multiple-choice question. Check which Paper 3 section your school prepares."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Explain what Jesus meant when he said, 'Give to Caesar what is Caesar's and to God what is God's' (Mark 12:17).",
           "Plan: (1) the setting: opponents tried to trap Jesus about the Roman tax; (2) the coin carried Caesar's image, so it could be returned to him; (3) Jesus does not deny the state's right to taxes; (4) but 'to God what is God's' gives God the greater claim; (5) scholars debate whether it sets two spheres or ranks God's claim above all others."),
        PQ("Describe two teachings of Jesus on wealth.",
           "For example: (1) You cannot serve God and money (Matthew 6:24); store up treasure in heaven, because wealth can control the heart. (2) The rich man who could not give up his possessions (Mark 10:17-31): Jesus says it is hard for the rich to enter the kingdom, and those who leave everything for him are rewarded. Candidates may use the rich fool or Lazarus."),
        PQ("Examine Paul's teaching on marriage and singleness in 1 Corinthians 7.",
           "Use the structure in Worked example 2: Corinthian setting, marriage is good with mutual duties, singleness is a gift, rules on divorce and mixed marriage, and the reason of the present distress. Add a short evaluation: Paul gives his own judgement at times ('I say, not the Lord') and distinguishes it from the Lord's command, so Christians debate how to apply it today."),
        SUM("Mark 12 and Romans 13 call for respect for the state and taxes; Acts 5:29 and Revelation 13 set limits.",
            "Scholars read Romans 13 as advice for a time and place or as a general principle; check your textbook.",
            "Jesus warns against serving money and trusting in riches; the early church shared goods; the love of money is the danger.",
            "Jesus points to creation in his teaching on divorce; Matthew has an exception clause that scholars read in different ways.",
            "Paul in 1 Corinthians 7 values both marriage and singleness and answers real Corinthian questions."),
    ]))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3. Eschatology and the Parousia
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_ETH, topic="Eschatology and the Parousia",
    title="Eschatology and the Parousia: realized and futuristic views",
    slug="rsu2-eschatology-parousia",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Eschatology means teaching about the last things. This lesson explains the Parousia (the return of Christ), the Gospel and Pauline texts about it, and the scholarly views called realized, futuristic and inaugurated eschatology.",
    objectives=[
        "Define eschatology, apocalyptic and Parousia and say why the subject matters in the New Testament",
        "Describe what Mark 13, 1 Thessalonians 4 to 5 and 1 Corinthians 15 teach about the end",
        "Explain realized eschatology (C. H. Dodd), futuristic eschatology (Schweitzer and Weiss) and the 'already and not yet' view",
        "Evaluate how early Christians dealt with the delay of the Parousia",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Eschatology", "Parousia", "Apocalyptic", "Realized eschatology", "Futuristic eschatology", "Already and not yet", "Delay of the Parousia"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("In many Cameroonian churches you may hear a song or a sermon that ends with the words, 'Jesus is coming soon.' That hope is as old as the New Testament. The study of what Christians believe about the end of history, judgement and the final kingdom is called **eschatology**. The syllabus asks about two themes: the kingdom as something that has already arrived (realized) and something still to come (futuristic), and the **Parousia**, the return of Christ."),
        D("**Eschatology** is teaching about the last things: death, judgement, resurrection and God's final rule. The **Parousia** is a Greek word meaning 'presence' or 'arrival'. Christians use it for the second coming of Christ at the end. **Apocalyptic** is a kind of writing (for example Daniel and Revelation) that uses symbols and visions to speak of God's final victory.", title="Definitions"),
        H("The New Testament material"),
        KEY("Where to look", "Learn at least one passage in each group.", cards=[
            ["Mark 13", "The 'little apocalypse': Jesus warns of wars, false messiahs and persecution; speaks of the Son of Man coming; says that no one knows the day or hour, so disciples must watch."],
            ["1 Thessalonians 4:13-5:11", "Paul comforts Christians who worry about those who have died. He says Christ will return, those who have died in Christ will rise first, and the living will meet the Lord. The day will come like a thief, so keep awake."],
            ["1 Corinthians 15", "Paul links Christ's resurrection to the resurrection of believers at his coming, and to the end when God is all in all."],
            ["2 Thessalonians 2", "Some had said the day had come. Paul says certain events must come first and warns against being shaken."],
            ["2 Peter 3:3-9", "Mockers ask where the promised coming is. The writer says that the Lord is patient and that time is not measured as people measure it."],
            ["Matthew 25", "Parables of watchfulness and the judgement of the nations, where care for the needy is the test."],
        ]),
        T("Note how the texts have two aims. They give hope to those who suffer, and they call believers to be ready and live well. No text in the New Testament gives a date. Mark 13 says plainly that even the Son does not know it."),
        H("Three scholarly views"),
        KEY("Futuristic eschatology", "Johannes Weiss and Albert Schweitzer argued that Jesus preached a kingdom that would come soon by God's act, as in Jewish apocalyptic thought. In this view the kingdom is mainly future. Critics say that this ignores sayings which speak of the kingdom as present.", cards=[]),
        KEY("Realized eschatology", "C. H. Dodd argued that, in the ministry of Jesus, the kingdom of God has already arrived. The 'last things' were fulfilled in his coming, death and resurrection. The future language, in this reading, is a picture of the eternal truth. Critics say that Dodd explained away the many clear sayings about a future coming.", cards=[]),
        KEY("Inaugurated eschatology ('already and not yet')", "Many later scholars joined the two views. The kingdom has begun in Jesus, with signs such as healings and the gift of the Spirit, but it will be completed only at the end. Scholars such as Oscar Cullmann are linked with this approach; check your textbook for names your course uses.", cards=[]),
        IMG("alrsu2-eschatology-timeline.svg", alt="A timeline with the ministry and resurrection of Jesus on the left and the Parousia on the right, showing where realized, futuristic and already-not-yet views place the kingdom.", caption="Figure 1. Three views of when the kingdom comes."),
        T("Evidence for the present kingdom: Jesus says that if he drives out demons by the finger of God, then the kingdom has come upon people; he announces that the time is fulfilled and the kingdom has come near (Mark 1:15); and the Gospel of John speaks of eternal life as something believers already have (John 5:24). Evidence for the future kingdom: Mark 13, the prayer 'your kingdom come', the Son of Man coming in judgement, and the future resurrection in 1 Corinthians 15."),
        EX("Worked example 1: classify the evidence", intro="Place each saying in the best box: present or future. (a) 'The kingdom of God has come near' (Mark 1:15). (b) 'Your kingdom come' (Lord's Prayer). (c) 'Whoever hears my word has eternal life' (John 5:24). (d) 'They will see the Son of Man coming' (Mark 13:26).", steps=[
            S("(a) Mark 1:15", text="Present or beginning: the kingdom is close, active in Jesus' ministry."),
            S("(b) The Lord's Prayer", text="Future: believers pray for a coming that is not yet complete."),
            S("(c) John 5:24", text="Present: eternal life is already received by those who believe. Scholars call John strongly realized."),
            S("(d) Mark 13:26", text="Future: the Son of Man comes at the end."),
            S("Conclusion", text="Both present and future sayings exist, so the best explanation holds them together: already and not yet."),
        ]),
        H("The delay of the Parousia"),
        T("The first Christians expected Christ's return soon. When some believers died and Christ had not come, the question 'Why the delay?' arose. Scholars describe several responses in the New Testament. Paul says that the dead in Christ will not miss out (1 Thessalonians 4). Second Thessalonians says that certain events come first. Second Peter says that God is patient and gives time to repent. Luke gives the church a task in the world, as in the book of Acts, where the Spirit and mission fill the time before the end. John stresses what believers already have through faith."),
        D("**Delay of the Parousia** is the name for the problem that Christ did not return as early Christians expected, and the ways the New Testament authors responded. Some scholars, such as Schweitzer, say that it caused a crisis. Others say that it was a smaller issue because the Gospels already warn that no one knows the time.", title="Definition"),
        MISTAKE("Giving a date", "Never say that the New Testament teaches that Christ will come in a given year. Mark 13:32 says that only the Father knows. Examiners expect candidates to explain the teaching, not to predict."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up the views", "Realized means 'already here', futuristic means 'still to come'. The 'already and not yet' view says both. Learn who goes with which: Dodd with realized; Weiss and Schweitzer with futuristic."),
        QUIZ("Which scholar is linked with realized eschatology?", ["Albert Schweitzer", "Johannes Weiss", "C. H. Dodd", "Oscar Cullmann"], 2,
             "Dodd argued that the kingdom had arrived in the ministry of Jesus.", hint="Think of the word 'realized'.",
             feedback=["Schweitzer is linked with futuristic eschatology.", "Weiss is linked with futuristic eschatology.", "", "Cullmann is linked with the 'already and not yet' approach."]),
        QUIZ("Which gospel stresses that eternal life is already a present gift to believers?", ["Mark", "John", "Luke only", "None of the Gospels"], 1,
             "John 5:24 speaks of those who believe having passed from death to life.", hint="Think of the Fourth Gospel.",
             feedback=["Mark has much future language, in chapter 13.", "", "Luke also teaches about the Spirit and mission, not only this.", "John clearly has it."]),
        TIP("Exam link. Eschatology can appear as a structural question in Paper 3 (for example on the Parousia in 1 Thessalonians) or as multiple-choice questions on key terms. A strong answer defines the term, uses a text and names the scholar if one is asked."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("(a) What is meant by the Parousia? (b) State two New Testament passages that speak of it.",
           "(a) The Parousia is the Greek word for presence or arrival, used for Christ's coming at the end of the age. (b) For example: Mark 13, 1 Thessalonians 4:13-5:11, 1 Corinthians 15:23, Matthew 25."),
        PQ("Distinguish realized eschatology from futuristic eschatology.",
           "Realized (Dodd): the kingdom has already come in Jesus, so the 'last things' are fulfilled in his ministry, death and resurrection. Futuristic (Weiss, Schweitzer): the kingdom is mainly future and was expected soon, by God's act. Add one text for each and note that the inaugurated view joins the two."),
        PQ("Discuss how the New Testament deals with the delay of the Parousia.",
           "Plan: (1) the early expectation and the problem when believers died; (2) Paul's comfort in 1 Thessalonians; (3) 2 Thessalonians says events must come first; (4) 2 Peter says God is patient; (5) Luke and John shift emphasis to mission and present life; (6) evaluation: Mark 13 already says that no one knows the time, so the delay may not have been a crisis."),
        SUM("Eschatology means teaching about the last things; the Parousia is the return of Christ.",
            "Mark 13, 1 Thessalonians, 1 Corinthians 15 and 2 Peter give hope and a call to be ready; none gives a date.",
            "Dodd stressed that the kingdom had come (realized); Weiss and Schweitzer stressed the future; many scholars hold both (already and not yet).",
            "The New Testament answers the delay of the Parousia with comfort, patience, mission and present life in the Spirit."),
    ]))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 4. The Reformation
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_ALT, topic="The Reformation",
    title="The Reformation: Luther, Zwingli, Calvin, England and Trent",
    slug="rsu2-the-reformation",
    minutes=25,
    summary="The sixteenth-century movement that divided Western Christianity: Luther's challenge, the Swiss reformers Zwingli and Calvin, the English Reformation and the Catholic response at the Council of Trent.",
    objectives=[
        "Explain the causes of the Reformation and the main ideas of Martin Luther",
        "Compare Luther, Zwingli and Calvin on authority, the Lord's Supper and church life",
        "Outline the English Reformation under Henry VIII and his successors",
        "Describe the Catholic Counter-Reformation and the work of the Council of Trent",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Indulgences", "Justification by faith", "Scripture alone", "Priesthood of all believers", "Predestination", "Act of Supremacy", "Council of Trent", "Counter-Reformation"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Many churches in Cameroon trace their roots to the sixteenth-century Reformation: the Presbyterian, Lutheran, Baptist and Anglican traditions all come from it, and the Catholic Church was also changed by it. Missionaries who brought Christianity to Cameroon came from those traditions, so this lesson also helps with the next one. The Reformation is a European story, so we need to be careful with dates. Check your textbook where I say so."),
        T(TRACK),
        H("Causes"),
        T("Historians point to several causes. **Religious**: many people felt that the church had lost its focus, that some clergy were uneducated or worldly, and that the practice of selling **indulgences** (certificates that reduced the punishment for sin) had become a scandal. **Intellectual**: the Renaissance encouraged return to original sources, and the printing press (about the 1450s) spread ideas fast. Scholars such as Erasmus published a Greek New Testament in 1516. **Political**: rulers in Germany and elsewhere resented the pope's power and wealth, and some saw advantage in independence."),
        H("Martin Luther"),
        D("**Martin Luther** (1483-1546) was a German monk and professor at Wittenberg. In 1517 he wrote the **Ninety-five Theses**, an invitation to debate about indulgences, traditionally dated to 31 October 1517. The theses spread quickly in print. In 1521 he refused to take back his writings at the **Diet of Worms** and was put under the ban of the Empire, but protected by the ruler of Saxony. He then translated the New Testament into German (published 1522).", title="Definition"),
        KEY("Luther's central ideas", "Luther's teaching changed how many Christians saw salvation and authority.", cards=[
            ["Justification by faith", "A person is made right with God by faith in Christ and by God's grace, not by good works or buying indulgences. He read Paul's letters, especially Romans, in this way."],
            ["Scripture alone", "The Bible, not the pope or church councils, is the final authority. This is why Luther urged that it be translated for ordinary people."],
            ["Priesthood of all believers", "Every baptised Christian has access to God. The clergy have a role of service, not a higher spiritual rank."],
            ["Two sacraments", "Luther kept baptism and the Lord's Supper as sacraments, because Christ instituted them."],
        ]),
        H("Zwingli and Calvin"),
        T("**Ulrich Zwingli** (1484-1531) began preaching reform in Zurich from 1519. He stressed the Bible as sole authority and removed images, the Mass and other practices that he could not find in Scripture. He and Luther agreed on many points but disagreed on the Lord's Supper. Luther held that Christ's body and blood are truly present, while Zwingli taught that the bread and wine are signs that recall Christ's sacrifice. They met at Marburg in 1529 and could not agree on this point. Zwingli died in battle at Kappel in 1531."),
        T("**John Calvin** (1509-1564) was a French reformer. His book, *Institutes of the Christian Religion* (first edition 1536), set out Protestant teaching in an ordered form. He worked in Geneva, where he led a church whose life was shaped by Scripture, a strict moral discipline and an educated ministry. Calvin stressed the sovereignty of God and taught **predestination**: God chooses some for salvation by his free will. This teaching has been debated inside and outside the Reformed tradition. His church order, with elders and ministers sharing leadership, is the root of the Presbyterian system."),
        KEY("Comparing the three", "Compare them on the same points in an essay.", cards=[
            ["Authority", "All three put Scripture above tradition. Luther was more willing to keep traditions not clearly against Scripture, Zwingli and Calvin removed more."],
            ["Lord's Supper", "Luther: real presence. Zwingli: remembrance and sign. Calvin: spiritual presence by the Holy Spirit (a middle position)."],
            ["Church order", "Luther worked with rulers; Zwingli with the city council; Calvin built a church with its own discipline and elders."],
        ]),
        IMG("alrsu2-reformation-timeline.svg", alt="A timeline of the Reformation: 1517 Ninety-five Theses, 1521 Diet of Worms, 1529 Marburg, 1534 Act of Supremacy, 1536 Calvin's Institutes, 1545 to 1563 Council of Trent.", caption="Figure 1. Key dates of the Reformation (check your textbook for small differences)."),
        H("The English Reformation"),
        T("In England the break began with a political and personal issue as well as religious ones. King Henry VIII wanted his marriage to Catherine of Aragon ended, and the pope did not grant it. Parliament passed the **Act of Supremacy** (1534), which made the king head of the Church of England. Monasteries were closed and their property taken in the following years. Under Edward VI the church moved towards Protestant teaching, with the English **Book of Common Prayer** (first issued in 1549). Mary I restored Catholic practice, and many Protestants were executed. Under Elizabeth I the **Settlement** (about 1559) created a church that was Protestant in teaching and kept some older forms, a 'middle way'. Historians still debate how far ordinary people wanted these changes."),
        H("The Counter-Reformation"),
        D("The **Counter-Reformation** (or Catholic Reformation) was the Catholic Church's response. It included both reform from inside and defence against Protestants. The **Council of Trent** met in sessions from 1545 to 1563. It confirmed that Scripture and tradition are both authorities, that there are seven sacraments, that faith and works both matter for salvation, and that the Latin Vulgate is the standard text. It also ordered reforms: seminaries to train priests, bishops to live in their dioceses, and an end to abuses in the sale of indulgences. Check your textbook for the exact decrees your course names.", title="Definition"),
        T("New orders helped renewal. The Society of Jesus (the **Jesuits**), founded by Ignatius of Loyola and approved by the pope in 1540, became known for education and mission. Jesuit and other Catholic missionaries later travelled to Asia, the Americas and Africa."),
        EX("Worked example 1: answer a 'compare' question", intro="Compare the teaching of Luther and Zwingli on the Lord's Supper.", steps=[
            S("Say what they share", text="Both rejected the Catholic teaching that the bread and wine change substance, and both valued the Supper as a gift of Christ."),
            S("Luther", text="Christ's body and blood are really present with the bread and wine, because Christ said, 'This is my body'."),
            S("Zwingli", text="The words are figurative. The bread and wine are signs that help believers remember Christ's sacrifice."),
            S("Result", text="They met at Marburg in 1529 and agreed on most points, but not on the presence of Christ in the Supper."),
            S("Comment", text="Calvin offered a middle view: Christ is present spiritually."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Calling the Reformation only an English story", "The movement began in Germany and Switzerland, and the English Reformation was one national pattern among others. The report on Religious Studies says candidates must show knowledge of the syllabus content, not general ideas. Name the people, dates and decisions."),
        QUIZ("Which doctrine did Luther teach that a person is made right with God by faith?", ["Predestination", "Justification by faith", "Transubstantiation", "Papal supremacy"], 1,
             "Justification by faith, from Paul's teaching in Romans, was the centre of Luther's thought.", hint="Think of faith and grace.",
             feedback=["Predestination is linked more with Calvin.", "", "Transubstantiation is the Catholic teaching on the Lord's Supper.", "Luther rejected papal authority."]),
        QUIZ("What did the Act of Supremacy (1534) do?", ["Banned the Bible in English", "Made the English king head of the Church of England", "Closed the Council of Trent", "Made Calvin the leader in Geneva"], 1,
             "Parliament recognised Henry VIII as head of the church in England.", hint="It was passed in England.",
             feedback=["The English Bible was encouraged, not banned.", "", "Trent was a Catholic council and met later.", "Geneva is in Switzerland and is unrelated."]),
        TIP("Exam link. Church history topics belong to the Alternative Track. Candidates answering history-based questions need dates, names and decisions. When you evaluate, say that historians debate the causes and who gained most."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("State three reasons for the Reformation in the sixteenth century.",
           "For example: (1) abuses in the church such as the selling of indulgences; (2) the Renaissance and printing spread new learning and Bibles; (3) political desire of rulers for independence from the pope; (4) new teaching such as justification by faith by Luther."),
        PQ("Explain Luther's main teachings.",
           "Justification by faith alone through grace (Romans); Scripture alone as authority; priesthood of all believers; two sacraments; German Bible. Give a short context: the Ninety-five Theses (1517) began with indulgences, and Luther refused to take back his writings at Worms (1521)."),
        PQ("Assess the work of the Council of Trent.",
           "Plan: (1) it met between 1545 and 1563 in response to Protestants; (2) it defended Catholic doctrine on Scripture and tradition, seven sacraments and the mass; (3) it reformed practice with seminaries and bishops' duties; (4) assessment: it strengthened the Catholic Church and clarified teaching but made the split with Protestants permanent."),
        SUM("Causes of the Reformation include church abuses, new learning and printing, and politics.",
            "Luther taught justification by faith, Scripture alone and the priesthood of all believers; he began in 1517.",
            "Zwingli and Calvin led the Swiss Reformation; they differed on the Lord's Supper, and Calvin taught predestination.",
            "In England, the Act of Supremacy (1534) began the break from Rome and the Prayer Book shaped worship.",
            "The Council of Trent (1545-1563) defined Catholic teaching and reformed the clergy."),
    ]))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 5. Christianity in Cameroon
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_ALT, topic="Christianity in Cameroon",
    title="Christianity in Cameroon: the coming of the missions",
    slug="rsu2-christianity-in-cameroon",
    minutes=24,
    summary="An outline of how Christianity reached Cameroon in the nineteenth century (Baptist, Basel, Presbyterian, Catholic and other missions), what the missions did in education, health and language, and how churches became African-led.",
    objectives=[
        "Name the main missions that began work in Cameroon in the nineteenth century and say where they worked",
        "Describe the contribution of the missions to education, health and language",
        "Explain how the colonial changes after 1884 and 1916 affected the missions",
        "Evaluate the relationship between missions and colonial rule, and the rise of African-led churches",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Baptist Missionary Society", "Alfred Saker", "Basel Mission", "Catholic missions", "Presbyterian mission", "Mission schools", "African-led churches"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("In Cameroon, many families belong to a church with a mission history: Baptist along the coast, Presbyterian in the South West and North West, Catholic across the country, and evangelical churches in the French-speaking regions. Understanding that history helps explain why schools, hospitals and even some of our languages in print have church roots."),
        T("**Careful with details.** This lesson gives only the outline that historians widely agree on. Exact dates and the order of arrival differ between books and between regions, so we say 'check your textbook' where it matters. Use the dates your school uses in your own essays."),
        T(TRACK),
        H("The first missions on the coast"),
        T("Christianity reached the Cameroon coast in the nineteenth century through missions that came from Europe and the Caribbean. The British **Baptist Missionary Society** began work in the Gulf of Guinea region. The Jamaican Baptist missionary **Joseph Merrick** came from Fernando Po to the Cameroon coast in 1843 and worked at Bimbia. **Alfred Saker**, an English Baptist missionary, settled at Douala in 1845. He learned the Duala language, helped to translate the Bible into it, ran a school and a printing press, and in 1858 founded **Victoria** (now Limbe) as a settlement for freed slaves and Christian converts. Check your textbook for the exact dates."),
        KEY("The Baptist beginnings", "Remember who, where and what.", cards=[
            ["Joseph Merrick", "Jamaican missionary, came to Cameroon's coast in 1843, started work at Bimbia and Duala-speaking areas."],
            ["Alfred Saker", "Arrived at Douala in 1845, translated into Duala, founded Victoria in 1858."],
            ["What they did", "Preaching, schools, Bible translation, health care and a settlement for freed slaves."],
        ]),
        H("German rule and the Basel Mission"),
        T("In 1884 Germany declared a protectorate over the territory of Kamerun. The Baptist work was taken over in 1886 by the **Basel Mission**, a Protestant mission from Switzerland with German links. It expanded inland, into the grasslands, and became known for schools, trades such as carpentry and printing, and agricultural training. Its work among the peoples of the grassfields, including the Bali and Bamum areas, is part of church history; check your textbook for how your region's church began."),
        T("Catholic missions also began under German rule. The Pallottine Fathers arrived from Germany in 1890 and built stations and schools in several coastal and southern areas (check your textbook for details). An American Presbyterian mission was active in the southern forest areas, in the Batanga area, from about the late nineteenth century (check your textbook for the date)."),
        H("After the First World War"),
        T("When Germany lost its colonies in 1916 and 1919, Kamerun was divided between France and Britain. German missionaries were removed from their work. The **Paris Evangelical Mission** took over much of the Protestant work in the French zone, and the Basel Mission returned in the British zone after some years, with the **Mill Hill Missionaries** (a British Catholic society) taking Catholic work in the British zone from the 1920s. In the French zone the **Spiritans** (Holy Ghost Fathers) and other French Catholic missions expanded. Check your textbook for exact years and details."),
        TIP("The map of missions in your textbook will help you: place each mission on the coast, the grassfields, the forest of the south or the north, and note which colonial zone it belonged to after 1916."),
        H("What the missions contributed"),
        KEY("Contributions", "Examiners often ask for these. Give at least one example for each.", cards=[
            ["Education", "Mission schools taught reading, writing and trades. For many decades they trained many of the clerks, teachers and leaders in the country."],
            ["Health", "Mission hospitals and dispensaries gave care and trained nurses, and many still operate."],
            ["Language and the Bible", "Missions put local languages into writing, such as Duala, and translated the Bible and hymns."],
            ["Social change", "Missions opposed some practices such as the sale of people, and encouraged new patterns of family life, which sometimes caused tension."],
        ]),
        H("Missions, colonialism and African-led churches"),
        T("Historians debate the link between missions and colonial rule. Some say that missions helped colonial governments by teaching European values and language. Others say that missions also criticised colonial abuses and gave Africans education that later helped independence movements. Both views have evidence, so a good essay shows both. Some missionaries worked closely with the authorities, and others did not."),
        T("African Christians soon became more than receivers of mission teaching. Catechists, teachers, pastors and lay leaders carried the Gospel further than missionaries could. Over time the older missions became African-led churches. For example, the Presbyterian Church in Cameroon and the Cameroon Baptist Convention are today led by Cameroonians (check your textbook for the dates of autonomy). Other independent and Pentecostal churches also arose in the twentieth century. Candidates should avoid claiming numbers, since statistics differ."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a short answer on Alfred Saker", intro="Describe the work of Alfred Saker in Cameroon.", steps=[
            S("Who and when", text="An English Baptist missionary who settled at Douala in 1845 (check your textbook for the date your course gives)."),
            S("Language", text="He learned Duala and worked on translating the Bible."),
            S("Education and printing", text="He opened a school and a printing press to teach reading."),
            S("Community", text="In 1858 he founded Victoria for freed slaves and converts."),
            S("Significance", text="He is remembered as a founder of Christianity on the Cameroon coast."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up the missions", "Basel, Baptist, Presbyterian and Catholic missions are different. A frequent error is to attribute a station to the wrong mission. If you are unsure, say 'a Protestant mission' rather than guess, and check your textbook."),
        QUIZ("Which Baptist missionary settled at Douala in 1845 and founded Victoria in 1858?", ["Joseph Merrick", "Alfred Saker", "A Basel Mission teacher", "A Pallottine father"], 1,
             "Alfred Saker founded Victoria and translated parts of the Bible into Duala.", hint="He was English and Baptist.",
             feedback=["Merrick was Jamaican and began work at Bimbia in 1843.", "", "The Basel Mission took over later, in 1886.", "The Pallottines were Catholic and came in 1890."]),
        QUIZ("What was one result of the First World War for the missions in Cameroon?", ["All missions left Africa", "German missionaries were removed and other missions took over their work", "The Pope ordered all missions to close", "Missions began to charge tax"], 1,
             "Germany lost its colonies, so Britain and France administered the territory and other missions replaced the Germans.", hint="Think of what happened to German rule.",
             feedback=["Many missions stayed.", "", "No such order was made.", "Taxes were a government matter."]),
        TIP("Exam link. Church history in Cameroon is Alternative Track material. In essays name the mission, the place and the work, and always add an evaluation of one benefit and one criticism. Say 'check your textbook' in your own notes where you are unsure of a date."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Describe the arrival of Christianity in the coastal region of Cameroon.",
           "Baptist missionaries from Britain and the Caribbean came in the 1840s: Joseph Merrick (1843) and Alfred Saker (Douala, 1845). Saker translated the Bible into Duala, ran a school and press and founded Victoria in 1858. After 1884 the Basel Mission took over the Baptist work in 1886 under German rule. Catholic missions such as the Pallottines followed from 1890."),
        PQ("Discuss the contribution of the missions to education and health in Cameroon.",
           "Plan: (1) mission schools taught literacy and skills and educated many leaders; (2) hospitals and clinics provided care and trained nurses; (3) missions wrote local languages and translated the Bible; (4) evaluation: services were linked to conversion and to colonial systems, and the missions were not equal in all regions; (5) conclusion: significant, though mixed."),
        PQ("'The missions served colonial rule.' Evaluate this claim.",
           "Agree: missions arrived with or after colonial powers and used European languages; some cooperated with colonial administrators. Disagree: they educated Africans who later led independence movements, and some missionaries criticised abuses. Conclusion: relations varied by mission, place and time; check your textbook for examples."),
        SUM("The Baptist missions of Merrick (1843) and Saker (1845) began the work on the Cameroon coast.",
            "The Basel Mission took over in 1886 under German rule; the Pallottines began Catholic work in 1890.",
            "After 1916 the territory was divided between France and Britain, and other missions replaced the Germans.",
            "Missions contributed to education, health care and written languages, and the relationship with colonial rule is debated.",
            "Churches became African-led, and new independent and Pentecostal churches grew."),
    ]))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 6. Problem of evil and religious language
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_ALT, topic="The problem of evil and religious language",
    title="The problem of evil and religious language",
    slug="rsu2-evil-and-religious-language",
    minutes=25,
    summary="How can an all-powerful and loving God allow evil (Mackie, Augustine, Irenaeus and Hick, the free-will defence)? And can religious statements be meaningful (verification, falsification, analogy, symbol)?",
    objectives=[
        "Set out the logical and evidential problems of evil and the distinction between moral and natural evil",
        "Explain the Augustinian and Irenaean theodicies, and the free-will defence, with strengths and weaknesses",
        "Explain the verification and falsification challenges and responses from Hare, Mitchell, Aquinas and Tillich",
        "Evaluate whether religious language can be meaningful",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Theodicy", "Moral evil and natural evil", "Inconsistent triad", "Free-will defence", "Soul-making", "Verification principle", "Falsification", "Analogy and symbol"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A mother in a village loses a child to a flood. A pastor says, 'God has a plan.' The mother asks, 'How can this be if God is good?' People in every religion have asked such a question. Philosophers call it **the problem of evil**. A second challenge asks whether it even makes sense to talk about God at all. These two themes together form a core of the Philosophy of Religion, which is also a named section in the Board's Paper 3."),
        T(TRACK),
        H("The problem of evil"),
        D("**Moral evil** is suffering caused by human choices (war, theft, cruelty). **Natural evil** is suffering that has natural causes (earthquakes, disease, flood). A **theodicy** is an attempt to justify God's goodness in the face of evil.", title="Definitions"),
        KEY("The inconsistent triad", "J. L. Mackie (1955) argued that these three claims cannot all be true at once.", cards=[
            ["1", "God is all-powerful (omnipotent): he can prevent evil."],
            ["2", "God is all-good (omnibenevolent): he wants to prevent evil."],
            ["3", "Evil exists."],
        ]),
        IMG("alrsu2-evil-triad.svg", alt="A triangle with three claims: God is all-powerful, God is all-good, evil exists, and the note that the three seem hard to hold together.", caption="Figure 1. The problem of evil as an inconsistent triad."),
        T("This is the **logical problem of evil**: it says that belief in God is contradictory. Many philosophers, including some who are not religious, think that the logical problem is answered if there is a possible reason why God might allow evil, and the free-will defence gives one. The **evidential problem** (for example, in the work of William Rowe) is milder: it asks whether the amount and kind of evil, especially pointless suffering, makes God's existence unlikely."),
        H("Theodicies and defences"),
        KEY("Augustine", "Augustine of Hippo (354-430) taught that God made the world good, and evil is not a thing but a lack of good (a **privation**), like darkness as the absence of light. Moral evil came from human misuse of free will at the Fall, and natural evil results from a disordered world after the Fall. Suffering is also seen as just punishment. Critics: it blames humans for natural evil that existed before them, the idea of a literal Fall does not fit evolution, and a perfect being who can foresee a fall seems responsible.", cards=[]),
        KEY("Irenaeus and Hick", "Irenaeus (second century) taught that humans were created immature and grow towards God. John Hick developed this as the **soul-making** theodicy: a world with challenges and suffering lets people become mature moral persons in freedom. Critics: some suffering seems too severe or pointless, such as the suffering of infants, and not everyone seems to grow. Hick said that the process continues after death, a view that many find hopeful and others find speculative.", cards=[]),
        KEY("The free-will defence", "Alvin Plantinga argued that a world with free beings who sometimes choose wrongly can be better than a world of robots. If this is possible, there is no logical contradiction. Critics: it explains moral evil better than natural evil, and God might have made free beings who always choose right.", cards=[]),
        T("Other responses include the **process** idea that God is not all-powerful in the classical sense, and religious answers such as that of Job, where God does not explain but invites trust. In Christian thought the cross is also seen as God sharing in suffering. Islam and Judaism also treat suffering as a test and as part of a life under God's wisdom, though each has its own teaching; here we keep to the Christian discussion of the syllabus."),
        EX("Worked example 1: evaluate the free-will defence", intro="'The free-will defence solves the problem of evil.' Discuss.", steps=[
            S("Explain", text="God gives humans free will; real freedom allows wrong choices, so moral evil results from us, not from God."),
            S("Support", text="It answers the logical problem: it is possible that God has a good reason for permitting evil."),
            S("Challenge 1", text="It does not clearly explain natural evil such as earthquakes and disease."),
            S("Challenge 2", text="Could God have created free beings who always choose the good?"),
            S("Conclusion", text="It is strong against the logical problem, but weaker against natural evil and the evidential problem."),
        ]),
        H("Religious language"),
        T("The second theme asks how we can speak about God at all. If God is beyond space and time, words made for this world may fail. In the twentieth century, the **logical positivists**, a group of philosophers, held that a statement is meaningful only if it is true by definition (like maths) or can be checked by observation. A. J. Ayer popularised this **verification principle**. By this test, 'God exists' seems to have no meaning at all. Critics point out that the principle itself cannot be checked by observation, and that many meaningful statements, such as about the past, are hard to verify."),
        T("Antony Flew, drawing on a parable by John Wisdom, argued in the 1950s that religious claims face the problem of **falsification**: a claim that means something must rule something out. In the parable, two explorers find a clearing and one says that a gardener tends it. Every test fails to find the gardener, but the believer keeps adjusting the claim until it seems to say nothing; Flew called this 'death by a thousand qualifications'. R. M. Hare replied that religious claims are **bliks**, basic ways of seeing the world that shape life but are not tested like scientific claims. Basil Mitchell replied that believers do accept that evil counts against belief and keep trusting anyway, like a partisan who trusts a stranger who says he is on his side. Check your textbook for the details of each parable."),
        KEY("Other ways of understanding religious language", "Many religious thinkers say that these language types fit better than verification.", cards=[
            ["Via negativa", "Say only what God is not (not limited, not changing), because God is beyond description."],
            ["Analogy (Aquinas)", "Words like 'good' or 'wise' apply to God and to humans in a related but not identical way, so they are neither exactly the same nor completely different."],
            ["Symbol (Tillich)", "Paul Tillich held that religious language points beyond itself and shares in what it points to; it is more than a sign."],
            ["Language games (Wittgenstein)", "Meaning depends on use within a community with its own rules, so religious language should be judged in its own way."],
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Confusing the two challenges", "Verification says that a statement is meaningless unless it can be checked. Falsification says that a claim must say what would count against it. They are related but different; Flew's challenge is the one linked to the gardener parable."),
        QUIZ("What does the term 'privation' mean in Augustine's account of evil?", ["Evil is a created thing", "Evil is a lack of good", "Evil is an illusion with no effect", "Evil is created by the devil alone"], 1,
             "Augustine taught that evil is not a substance but the absence of good, as darkness is the absence of light.", hint="Think of a hole in a cloth.",
             feedback=["Augustine denied that evil is a created thing.", "", "He did not say that suffering is an illusion.", "His account emphasises misuse of free will."]),
        QUIZ("Which thinker replied to Flew's challenge by calling religious beliefs 'bliks'?", ["R. M. Hare", "A. J. Ayer", "J. L. Mackie", "Plantinga"], 0,
             "Hare called a blik a basic way of looking at the world that is not tested like a scientific claim.", hint="He replied to the gardener parable.",
             feedback=["", "Ayer promoted the verification principle.", "Mackie developed the inconsistent triad.", "Plantinga is linked with the free-will defence."]),
        TIP("Exam link. Philosophy of Religion is a named section of Paper 3, and the Board's report lists questions on the attributes of God, Kant, the five ways of Aquinas, Aristotle's influence, death, and the falsification principle. This lesson supports the evil and language topics, so use it with your lessons on arguments for God."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Distinguish moral evil from natural evil, with an example of each.",
           "Moral evil is caused by human choices, for example murder or theft. Natural evil comes from natural processes, for example an earthquake or disease. Both raise the problem of evil, but the free-will defence deals more easily with moral evil."),
        PQ("Explain Hick's soul-making theodicy.",
           "Hick followed Irenaeus: humans start immature and grow towards God through freely chosen responses to challenges. A world of difficulty and suffering is needed for moral growth. Evaluation: it makes sense of some suffering, but critics say that some evils are too extreme and that not everyone benefits."),
        PQ("'Religious language is meaningless.' Discuss.",
           "Plan: (1) the verification principle (Ayer) and falsification (Flew with the parable of the gardener); (2) replies: Hare (bliks), Mitchell (partisan and trust); (3) Aquinas' analogy and Tillich's symbol as ways in which language can be meaningful; (4) Wittgenstein's language games; (5) conclusion: the challenges show real problems, but verification itself fails its own test."),
        SUM("The problem of evil asks how an all-powerful, all-good God can allow evil; Mackie's triad is the logical form.",
            "Augustine says that evil is a privation and that it comes from the misuse of free will; Irenaeus and Hick say that suffering helps souls grow.",
            "The free-will defence (Plantinga) answers the logical problem better than natural evil.",
            "Verification and falsification challenge religious language; Hare, Mitchell, Aquinas and Tillich reply."),
    ]))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 7. Bioethics
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_ALT, topic="Bioethics",
    title="Bioethics: life, death and medicine",
    slug="rsu2-bioethics",
    minutes=25,
    summary="How religious and philosophical ethics deal with abortion, euthanasia, assisted reproduction, genetic engineering and transplants, using ethical theories such as utilitarianism, Kantian ethics, natural law and situation ethics.",
    objectives=[
        "Define bioethics, sanctity of life and quality of life",
        "Explain how utilitarian, Kantian, natural law and situation ethics approach a medical question",
        "Summarise Christian and Muslim teachings on abortion, euthanasia and reproductive technology without ranking either",
        "Apply the four principles of medical ethics to a short case and evaluate",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Bioethics", "Sanctity of life", "Quality of life", "Utilitarianism", "Natural law", "Double effect", "Four principles", "Euthanasia"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Imagine a doctor in a district hospital who must decide how to use the one ventilator available. Or a family asked whether to switch off the machine that keeps a relative alive. These decisions belong to **bioethics**: ethics about life, health and medicine. New medical technology, such as artificial reproduction and gene editing, makes such questions more urgent."),
        T("This lesson is for study. It describes religious and philosophical positions fairly and does not tell you what to believe. Real patients and families should always be helped by qualified medical staff and people they trust. Ethics and Morality is a named section of the Board's Paper 3, so it also helps with the Ethical Theories in your wider course."),
        T(TRACK),
        D("**Bioethics** is the study of moral questions raised by biology and medicine. The **sanctity of life** principle says that human life is sacred and has special value because of God's gift. The **quality of life** view says that the kind of life a person can live also matters when decisions are made.", title="Definitions"),
        H("Four ethical frameworks"),
        KEY("How each framework approaches a question", "Be ready to apply each to a case in an essay.", cards=[
            ["Utilitarianism", "The right action produces the greatest happiness or least suffering for the greatest number (Bentham, Mill). It looks at consequences, so it can support some decisions to end life if suffering is reduced. Critic: it can neglect the rights of individuals."],
            ["Kantian ethics", "Act only on rules that could be universal laws, and treat persons as ends in themselves, not only as means. Many Kantians reject killing, but accept that persons have rights to autonomy. Critic: rules can conflict."],
            ["Natural law", "Associated with Thomas Aquinas: humans have natural purposes, such as preserving life and raising children. Acts that go against these purposes are wrong. The doctrine of double effect allows an act with a good aim and a foreseen bad side effect in some conditions. Critic: it depends on a view of nature that some dispute."],
            ["Situation ethics", "Joseph Fletcher argued that the loving thing in each case is the only fixed rule, so each decision depends on context. Critic: it may allow almost anything, and it is hard to predict the result of love."],
        ]),
        H("Beginning of life"),
        T("**Abortion.** The central question is: when does a developing human life gain full moral status, and how should the rights of the mother and the unborn be balanced? The Roman Catholic Church teaches that life is to be protected from conception and opposes abortion. Other Christian groups differ: some allow it in cases such as danger to the mother's life or rape, and some give women more freedom of conscience. Many Muslim scholars teach that abortion is wrong after a certain stage of pregnancy (the 'ensoulment' stage is debated, often placed at 120 days in traditional teaching), and some permit it earlier for serious reasons such as danger to the mother. Views differ among scholars. Check your textbook for the positions your course gives. The law in each country sets its own limits, so check current law locally."),
        T("**Assisted reproduction.** In vitro fertilisation (IVF) helps couples who cannot conceive. Supporters stress the joy of children and the right to a family. Opponents, for example the Catholic Church, object that it separates conception from the marriage act, and that spare embryos may be destroyed. Many Muslim scholars allow assisted reproduction within marriage with the couple's own gametes, but not the use of donor gametes. Check your textbook."),
        H("End of life"),
        D("**Euthanasia** means ending a life to relieve suffering. **Voluntary** euthanasia is at the patient's request; **non-voluntary** is when the patient cannot consent; **active** means acting to cause death; **passive** means allowing death by withholding or withdrawing treatment. Assisted suicide is when a person is given the means to end their own life.", title="Definitions"),
        T("Christian traditions and Islam generally teach that life is a gift or trust from God, so deliberate killing, including active euthanasia, is wrong, while allowing death by stopping burdensome treatment can be acceptable (the doctrine of double effect applies to pain relief that may shorten life). Utilitarians may support voluntary euthanasia to prevent suffering. Opponents of this view worry about pressure on the weak (the 'slippery slope'). The legal position differs between countries."),
        H("Other questions"),
        T("**Organ transplants.** Many religious traditions see donation as an act of love, while some raise issues about respect for the body. **Genetic engineering and cloning.** Treating serious disease is widely welcomed, while 'designer babies' raise worries about equality and about humans taking God's place. **Stewardship**: many Christians and Muslims teach that humans are trustees of God's creation, so power over life carries responsibility."),
        KEY("The four principles", "Beauchamp and Childress proposed four principles used in medical ethics. They give a practical way to analyse any case.", cards=[
            ["Autonomy", "Respect the patient's right to choose."],
            ["Beneficence", "Act to do good for the patient."],
            ["Non-maleficence", "Avoid causing harm."],
            ["Justice", "Share resources and care fairly."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: apply the four principles", intro="A patient with a painful incurable disease, still mentally able, refuses further treatment. Analyse the case using the four principles.", steps=[
            S("Autonomy", text="The patient has the right to refuse treatment after being properly informed."),
            S("Beneficence", text="Doctors want to help, so they must discuss pain relief and care that improves comfort."),
            S("Non-maleficence", text="Forcing treatment the patient refuses may cause harm; but doctors must not act to end life directly."),
            S("Justice", text="Resources and care should be offered fairly, and the patient should receive proper palliative care."),
            S("Conclusion", text="The principles can point in different directions; the decision needs careful discussion and the law of the country should be followed."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("One-sided answers", "A common weakness is writing only your own opinion or listing only one religion's view. The Board's report asks for explanation of concepts and, in Paper 3 questions, application. Give the arguments for each side and then your judgement, clearly labelled."),
        QUIZ("Which ethical approach says that the right act is the one that produces the greatest happiness for the greatest number?", ["Natural law", "Kantian ethics", "Utilitarianism", "Divine command"], 2,
             "Utilitarianism judges acts by consequences and total happiness.", hint="Bentham and Mill.",
             feedback=["Natural law looks at natural purposes.", "Kantian ethics looks at universal duties.", "", "Divine command looks at God's commands."]),
        QUIZ("What does passive euthanasia mean?", ["Giving a lethal injection", "Allowing death by withholding or withdrawing treatment", "Helping someone to travel abroad", "Making a will"], 1,
             "Passive euthanasia means allowing a patient to die by not starting or by stopping treatment.", hint="Passive means not acting.",
             feedback=["That is active euthanasia.", "", "It is not what passive means.", "It is unrelated."]),
        TIP("Exam link. Ethics and Morality is a Paper 3 section in the Board's report, where candidates were asked about utilitarianism and human happiness, rules and freedom, evil and suffering, and the falsification principle. Use this lesson with your Ethics notes, and check which track your school teaches."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Explain the doctrine of the sanctity of life.",
           "Life is sacred because it is a gift from God, made in God's image in Jewish and Christian thought, and a trust from God in Islam. For that reason, human life has special value that others must respect and ending it is wrong except in rare cases. Evaluation: critics ask whether sanctity should outweigh suffering and the wishes of a competent patient."),
        PQ("Compare how a utilitarian and a natural law thinker might respond to euthanasia.",
           "Utilitarian: judges by results, so voluntary euthanasia may be right if it reduces suffering and does not cause greater harm. Natural law: preserving life is a basic purpose, so deliberately ending life is wrong, though withdrawing burdensome treatment and the double effect of pain relief may be allowed. Evaluate: utilitarianism may allow pressure on the vulnerable; natural law depends on a contested view of nature."),
        PQ("Discuss the claim that religion should have no say in medical decisions.",
           "Plan: (1) agree: patients' autonomy, and a diverse society where not everyone shares a religion; (2) disagree: religion offers moral wisdom about life, hospitals run by churches and mosques, and many patients are religious; (3) apply the four principles; (4) conclusion: religious voices contribute to public debate, but decisions must respect law and patient choice."),
        SUM("Bioethics deals with moral questions in biology and medicine; sanctity of life and quality of life are two key ideas.",
            "Utilitarianism, Kantian ethics, natural law and situation ethics give different answers to the same case.",
            "Christian and Muslim teachings generally value life as a gift from God but differ in detail; always report them fairly.",
            "The four principles (autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, justice) help analyse cases."),
    ]))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 8. Islamic theology and jurisprudence
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_ALT, topic="Islamic theology and jurisprudence",
    title="Islamic theology (kalam) and jurisprudence (Sharia and fiqh)",
    slug="rsu2-islamic-theology-jurisprudence",
    minutes=25,
    summary="How Muslim scholars reasoned about God, free will and the Quran (kalam), and how Islamic law is derived from the Quran, the Sunna, consensus and analogy, with the four Sunni schools of law.",
    objectives=[
        "Explain tawhid and the main schools of kalam (Mu'tazila, Ash'ari, Maturidi) and their disagreements",
        "Distinguish Sharia from fiqh and name the four sources of Islamic law",
        "Name the four Sunni schools of law and their founders, and the Shia school (Ja'fari)",
        "Describe ijtihad and the five categories of acts, and give a balanced evaluation",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Tawhid", "Kalam", "Mu'tazila", "Ash'ari", "Sharia", "Fiqh", "Ijma", "Qiyas", "Ijtihad", "Madhhab"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("In northern Cameroon and in many towns, Muslims gather for Friday prayer and many ask scholars about daily questions: how to pay zakat, how to conduct a marriage, what is permitted in business. At the same time, believers have asked deeper questions about God: how can God be one, is a person free if God knows everything, is the Quran created or eternal? The first set of questions belongs to **jurisprudence**; the second to **theology**. This lesson introduces both."),
        T("The Board's Paper 3 has a section on the Historical Development of Islam and its Paper 2 asks about festivals, pillars and the role of women in Islam. This lesson goes into the later scholarly tradition, and fits only if your school teaches the Islam track. It describes what Muslims believe, in line with the rules of this course, and does not rank religions."),
        T(TRACK),
        H("Tawhid and theology"),
        D("**Tawhid** is the Islamic belief in the oneness of God (Allah): God is one, with no partner. **Kalam** (literally 'speech') is Islamic theology, the discipline that uses reasoning to explain and defend belief.", title="Definitions"),
        T("Early debates arose in the first centuries after the Prophet Muhammad. Scholars asked about faith and sin, about divine attributes (does God have a real hand and face, or are these figures of speech?), about free will and divine decree (**qadar**), and about the Quran. The main schools of kalam are given below. Dates and details vary in books, so check your textbook."),
        KEY("Three schools of kalam", "Learn the name, the emphasis and one disagreement for each.", cards=[
            ["Mu'tazila", "Emphasised reason and God's justice. Held that humans create their own acts, so they are responsible, and that the Quran was created in time. Their view of the created Quran was backed by the caliph al-Ma'mun in the 9th century (the 'inquisition' or mihna), a time of conflict."],
            ["Ash'ari", "Founded by al-Ash'ari (about the 10th century). Emphasised God's power and will; taught that God creates all acts, while humans 'acquire' them (kasb), and that the Quran is God's uncreated speech. Became widely accepted among Sunni Muslims."],
            ["Maturidi", "Founded by al-Maturidi, also in the 10th century. Gives reason a larger role than the Ash'aris and gives humans more real choice. Widely followed in the Hanafi tradition."],
        ]),
        T("Another debate was between those who rely on reason and those who stay close to the text, such as Ahmad ibn Hanbal, who refused to say that the Quran was created. Al-Ghazali (about 1058-1111) is a great figure who defended orthodox belief and combined theology, law and Sufi spirituality; check your textbook for details."),
        H("Sharia and fiqh"),
        D("**Sharia** means the 'path' or the way set by God, which includes belief, worship and conduct. **Fiqh** is Islamic jurisprudence, the human effort of scholars to understand and apply the Sharia to real questions. Sharia, in this sense, is God's way, and fiqh is the scholars' understanding of it, so differences between fiqh schools do not mean the Sharia itself is divided.", title="Definitions"),
        IMG("alrsu2-islamic-law-sources.svg", alt="Four stacked boxes showing sources of Islamic law in order: Quran, Sunna, ijma (consensus), qiyas (analogy).", caption="Figure 1. The four classical sources of Islamic law (Sunni)."),
        KEY("The four sources", "In classical Sunni law they are used in this order.", cards=[
            ["The Quran", "The word of God, the first source. It contains some direct rulings and many principles."],
            ["The Sunna", "The example of the Prophet, known through the collections of Hadith (his sayings and actions). Scholars study the chain of narrators to judge how reliable a report is."],
            ["Ijma", "Consensus of scholars (or of the community) on a point of law."],
            ["Qiyas", "Analogy: extending a ruling to a new case with the same cause (illa), for example the ban on intoxicants applied to new drugs."],
        ]),
        H("Schools of law and ijtihad"),
        KEY("The four Sunni schools", "Each school (madhhab) is named after a scholar. They agree on the basics and differ on details.", cards=[
            ["Hanafi", "Abu Hanifa (died 767). Gives a large role to reasoning, including analogy."],
            ["Maliki", "Malik ibn Anas (died 795). Emphasises the practice of the people of Medina. Widely followed in West and North Africa."],
            ["Shafi'i", "Al-Shafi'i (died 820). Gave a clear account of the sources of law and strengthened the authority of Hadith."],
            ["Hanbali", "Ahmad ibn Hanbal (died 855). Stays close to Quran and Hadith."],
        ]),
        T("Shia Muslims have their own tradition of law, the most common being the Ja'fari school, linked to the sixth Imam, Ja'far al-Sadiq. Dates and details differ in books, so check your textbook. West and Central African Muslims, including many in Cameroon, mostly follow the Maliki school; check your textbook for the local picture."),
        D("**Ijtihad** is independent reasoning by a qualified scholar to reach a ruling on a question not clearly settled by the texts. Muslims differ on whether the 'gate of ijtihad' closed in the Middle Ages, and many modern scholars say that ijtihad is still needed for new questions such as medical ethics and banking. Scholars also speak of the **maqasid** (aims) of the Sharia, such as protecting faith, life, mind, family and property.", title="Definition"),
        T("Fiqh classifies acts into five categories: **wajib** (obligatory), **mandub** (recommended), **mubah** (permitted), **makruh** (disliked) and **haram** (forbidden)."),
        EX("Worked example 1: ruling by qiyas", intro="Explain how qiyas could apply the ban on wine to a new intoxicating drink.", steps=[
            S("The original case", text="The Quran and Sunna forbid wine."),
            S("The cause (illa)", text="Scholars identify the cause as intoxication, which clouds the mind."),
            S("The new case", text="A new drink also intoxicates."),
            S("The ruling", text="By analogy the same ruling applies: the new drink is forbidden."),
            S("Evaluation", text="Qiyas allows law to deal with new situations, but critics warn that identifying the cause needs care, and schools differ in how much they use analogy."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Treating Sharia as a single code book", "The Sharia is God's way. The detailed rules in books are fiqh, produced by scholars of different schools. Candidates who say 'Sharia law is one fixed code' lose accuracy."),
        QUIZ("Which source of Islamic law comes first in order?", ["Qiyas", "Ijma", "The Quran", "Personal opinion"], 2,
             "The Quran is the first source, followed by the Sunna, ijma and qiyas.", hint="It is God's word.",
             feedback=["Qiyas is the fourth source.", "Ijma is the third source.", "", "Personal opinion is not among the four classical sources."]),
        QUIZ("Which school of kalam held that humans create their own acts and that the Quran was created?", ["Ash'ari", "Mu'tazila", "Hanbali", "Maturidi"], 1,
             "The Mu'tazila stressed God's justice and human responsibility, and held the Quran created.", hint="Think of reason and justice.",
             feedback=["The Ash'aris taught that the Quran is uncreated.", "", "The Hanbali is a law school and opposed the created Quran.", "The Maturidi school did not teach a created Quran."]),
        TIP("Exam link. The Board's report names Islam in Paper 2 (festivals, prophets, women, mercy, Hajj) and the Historical Development of Islam in Paper 3 (pre-Islamic ignorance, the Night Journey, the Quran's compilation, the Farewell Sermon, the death of the Prophet, Abu Bakr). This lesson is for the Islam track, and gives the later theology and law that sit behind those topics. Check which track your school teaches."),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Distinguish Sharia from fiqh.",
           "Sharia is God's way or path for human life, set out in the Quran and the Sunna. Fiqh is the human science of understanding and applying that way, developed by scholars and schools of law. Differences between schools are differences of fiqh, not of the revelation."),
        PQ("Name the four sources of Islamic law and explain one.",
           "The Quran, the Sunna, ijma and qiyas. Example: qiyas is reasoning by analogy, extending a ruling from a text to a new case that shares the same cause, for example applying the ban on wine to other intoxicants."),
        PQ("Discuss the view that Islamic law can deal with modern problems.",
           "Plan: (1) agree: ijtihad and the maqasid allow scholars to reason about new questions such as banking and medicine; (2) the diversity of schools gives flexibility; (3) disagree or qualify: scholars debate how much freedom ijtihad has and who is qualified; (4) conclusion: many Muslims hold that the tradition can address new questions, but there are real disagreements."),
        SUM("Tawhid is the belief in the oneness of God; kalam is Islamic theology, with Mu'tazila, Ash'ari and Maturidi schools.",
            "Sharia is God's way; fiqh is the scholars' understanding of it.",
            "The four sources are the Quran, the Sunna, ijma and qiyas.",
            "The four Sunni schools are Hanafi, Maliki, Shafi'i and Hanbali; the Ja'fari school is the main Shia school.",
            "Ijtihad is independent reasoning; acts are classed as wajib, mandub, mubah, makruh or haram."),
    ]))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 9. Paper 1
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_EXAM, topic="Paper 1",
    title="Paper 1 strategy: 50 multiple-choice questions",
    slug="rsu2-paper-1-strategy",
    minutes=20,
    summary="How to prepare for and answer the multiple-choice paper: what the Board's report says it tests, how to revise by comparing religions, and how to use the elimination method on options that sound right.",
    objectives=[
        "State the structure and purpose of Paper 1 as the Board's 2023 report describes it",
        "Revise key terms and facts in a comparative way across the religions in the syllabus",
        "Use elimination and the 'who, what, where, why' check on a multiple-choice question",
        "Avoid the typical traps of similar options and absolute words",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Paper 1 structure", "Comparative study", "Elimination", "Key terms", "Distractors", "Time management"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Paper 1 is the only paper with options to choose from rather than essays to write. It is quick to mark and quick to take, but it covers the whole course. That is why many candidates feel surprised: a question may ask about an Old Testament prophet, then a pillar of Islam, then an African religious belief, one after another."),
        H("What the Board's report says"),
        D("In the 2023 Advanced Level examination, **Paper 1** had **50 multiple-choice questions**, each carrying one mark, and made up about **34%** of the examination. The report says the questions tested the candidates' grasp of the **fundamental concepts of the world religions in the syllabus**. Papers 2 and 3 carry about 33% each.", title="Paper 1 in the Board's report"),
        T("The report also describes the level of the whole examination: it expects application and analysis, and the higher skills of synthesis and evaluation, according to Bloom's taxonomy. Even a multiple-choice question can test application, for example by describing a situation and asking which teaching it matches. The Board's advice to candidates is to study widely and to try comparative study of the religions, because that helps with Paper 1. The papers are different in style, so the checking of the exact format with your teacher is always wise."),
        TIP("The KKEVO mock exams are practice settings. The time (90 minutes) and the layout are our own, and your real timetable may differ. Check the instructions on your actual paper."),
        H("How to revise for Paper 1"),
        KEY("Four revision habits", "Use these with the lessons in your course.", cards=[
            ["Key terms list", "For each topic write the key terms with a one-line meaning: covenant, hesed, justification, Parousia, tawhid, zakat, ancestor, and so on."],
            ["Who, what, where, why", "For every person or event note the person, what they did, where, and why it matters. Many questions ask for exactly one of these."],
            ["Compare", "Put similar ideas side by side: prophets in Israel and in Islam, the Exodus and the Hijra, festivals of Christianity and Islam, sacred places across religions."],
            ["Practise with feedback", "After each practice set read why wrong options are wrong. The wrong options show the traps examiners use."],
        ]),
        H("Answering a question"),
        EX("Worked example 1: the elimination method", intro="Which prophet is known for the message that God desires justice and righteousness more than festivals and sacrifice? A. Elijah B. Amos C. Ezekiel D. Jeremiah", steps=[
            S("Read the stem", text="Underline the clue: justice and righteousness, not ritual."),
            S("Recall", text="This is the theme of social justice, linked with the shepherd-prophet from Tekoa."),
            S("Eliminate", text="Elijah is known for the contest on Mount Carmel and for opposing Baal worship. Ezekiel is known for the exile and the dry bones. Jeremiah is known for the new covenant."),
            S("Choose", text="Amos, option B."),
            S("Check", text="Re-read the stem to be sure that the answer matches every clue."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a trap of similar options", intro="Which statement about the Lord's Supper in Zwingli's teaching is correct? A. Christ's body is physically present B. The bread and wine are symbols that remember Christ's sacrifice C. The bread becomes Christ's body in substance D. Only the clergy may share", steps=[
            S("Identify the thinker and topic", text="Zwingli, the Lord's Supper."),
            S("Link to what you know", text="Zwingli taught remembrance, with signs."),
            S("Eliminate", text="A is Luther's real-presence view, C is the Catholic teaching, D is not Zwingli's teaching."),
            S("Choose", text="B."),
        ]),
        KEY("Common traps", "Learn these so that they do not catch you.", cards=[
            ["Right fact, wrong person", "An option may be true of Amos but placed under Hosea. Match the person to the fact."],
            ["Absolute words", "'Always', 'only' and 'never' often make an option false, because religious teachings have nuance. They are not automatically wrong."],
            ["Similar names", "Judges and kings, Luke and Acts, Sunna and Sharia. Take a second to read each name."],
            ["True but irrelevant", "An option may be true but not answer the question asked. Read the question again."],
        ]),
        H("Managing the time"),
        T("With 50 questions, give yourself about one minute for each one in a practice setting, and use the remaining time to return to questions you skipped. Never leave an answer blank when there is no penalty described in the instructions; make the best choice after eliminating. Check your real paper's instructions. Mark questions that you are unsure of and come back to them."),
        MISTAKE("Revising only your favourite section", "The report calls Paper 1 a test of the fundamental concepts across the syllabus. A candidate who has studied only the Old Testament will find the Islam and African Traditional Religion questions hard. Spread your revision across every module."),
        QUIZ("What does the Board's report say about the coverage of Paper 1?", [
            "It tests only the Old Testament", "It tests the fundamental concepts of the world religions in the syllabus", "It consists of essay questions", "It tests only African Traditional Religion"], 1,
            "The report says it tested the fundamental concepts of the world religions in the syllabus.", hint="Paper 1 is not an essay paper.",
            feedback=["It covers the whole syllabus.", "", "It is a multiple-choice paper.", "It covers all the religions."]),
        QUIZ("In a multiple-choice question, what should you do first?", [
            "Choose the longest option", "Read the question and think of the answer before looking at the options", "Skip it", "Pick option C"], 1,
            "Thinking of the answer first protects you from options built to sound right.", hint="Do the thinking before you read the options.",
            feedback=["Length is not a clue.", "", "Skipping is only for questions that take too long.", "Guessing without reasoning wastes the chance to eliminate."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("State how many questions Paper 1 has, and what kind they are.", "Fifty multiple-choice questions, as in the Board's 2023 report (check the format with your teacher for your session)."),
        PQ("Explain how comparing religions can help with Paper 1.",
           "Many questions test basic ideas such as covenant, prophecy, worship and festivals. Comparing the way different religions deal with these helps candidates to separate similar-sounding ideas and to remember what is distinctive in each. The Board's report also recommends comparative study."),
        PQ("A question asks, 'Which is the Muslim festival of sacrifice?' Describe the steps you would take to find the answer.",
           "Read the stem and underline 'festival of sacrifice'. Recall: Id-ul-Adha (Tabaski) with the sacrifice of an animal remembering Ibrahim's submission. Eliminate options that are another feast, such as the one that ends the fast of Ramadan. Check the answer against the stem."),
        SUM("Paper 1 has 50 multiple-choice questions and about 34% of the marks, according to the Board's 2023 report.",
            "It tests the fundamental concepts of the world religions across the syllabus.",
            "Revise key terms, use who-what-where-why, compare religions and learn from the wrong options.",
            "Read the stem first, eliminate, and watch for traps such as similar names and absolute words."),
    ]))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 10. Paper 2
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_EXAM, topic="Paper 2",
    title="Paper 2 strategy: choosing a section and writing the essay",
    slug="rsu2-paper-2-strategy",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Paper 2 asks for four essays from one section out of three. This lesson explains the structure, how to choose a section, and how to avoid the main fault the Board's report names: narrating instead of explaining significance.",
    objectives=[
        "State the structure of Paper 2 as the Board's 2023 report describes it",
        "Choose a section and four questions wisely",
        "Plan an essay that explains significance, not only the story",
        "Use context, evidence and evaluation in a religious studies essay",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Paper 2 structure", "Choosing a section", "Significance", "Command words", "Plan", "Evaluation"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("In a market, a seller who only says 'I have tomatoes' sells less than the seller who explains why her tomatoes are fresh, where they came from and what dish they suit. The same is true in a Religious Studies essay. The facts must be there, but the examiner wants to see why they matter. The Board's report returns to this point again and again."),
        H("The structure of Paper 2"),
        D("According to the Board's 2023 report, **Paper 2** had **18 essay questions** in **three sections of six questions**. Candidates were told to answer **four questions, all from one section**. The paper carries about **33%** of the examination, and the report gives the marks for each answer as out of 25. In 2023 the sections were the Old Testament (the Patriarchs to David), Islam, and African Traditional Religious Beliefs and Practices. Check which sections your school prepares and what your teacher expects in your session.", title="Paper 2 in the Board's report"),
        T("Because you answer all four from one section, you must **choose your section early** and revise it deeply. The report describes how many candidates in a section were able to attempt a popular question while very few attempted others; it also shows that even candidates who knew the story often scored low because they did not explain its significance."),
        H("Choosing the section and the questions"),
        KEY("What to weigh", "Make these decisions in the first minutes.", cards=[
            ["Your preparation", "Choose the section where at least four of the six questions are ones you can answer well. Do not choose after seeing only one question."],
            ["The command word", "Describe, explain, discuss, examine, assess, evaluate: each asks for a different job."],
            ["The focus", "What exact theme is asked? An essay on Jacob's role is not an essay on all the patriarchs."],
            ["Your evidence", "Can you name people, events, places and teachings? If not, choose another question."],
        ]),
        H("What the report says went wrong"),
        T("The report's comments, paraphrased, point to the same faults across sections."),
        KEY("Typical faults", "These come directly from the comments on candidates' scripts.", cards=[
            ["Narrating instead of explaining significance", "On the Exodus, many candidates told the story and did not show why it was central to Israel's identity and faith. On the festivals of Islam, many described the practices but did not explain the importance."],
            ["Missing the focus", "A question about a particular figure or period was answered with general material. A question about the judges needed names and roles, but candidates could not name the leaders. A question on the confederacy needed its dynamics, but many wrote only about Saul's rejection."],
            ["Confusing categories", "In African Traditional Religion, candidates mixed up sacred places with ordinary social places."],
            ["Drifting to other topics", "On a question about one Muslim feast, candidates mentioned other feasts instead of staying with the one asked."],
            ["No application to the context", "Answers that were not placed in the setting of the religion or time of the question lost marks."],
        ]),
        H("A reliable essay plan"),
        KEY("Five parts", "Use the same frame each time, and adjust it to the question.", cards=[
            ["Introduction", "Define the key term or set the scene in two or three sentences, and say what you will argue."],
            ["Points", "Three or four points, each with evidence: a name, an event, a teaching, a text or a practice."],
            ["Significance", "For each point say why it matters for the religion or the believers."],
            ["Evaluation", "Where the question says 'discuss' or 'assess', give another view or a limit."],
            ["Conclusion", "Answer the question directly in two sentences."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: turn narration into significance", intro="Question (written for practice): 'Explain the importance of the Exodus in the religion of Israel.'", steps=[
            S("Narration (weak)", text="'The Israelites were slaves in Egypt, and Moses went to Pharaoh, and the sea opened.' This tells the story."),
            S("Significance 1: deliverance", text="God freed a people from bondage, so Israel saw Yahweh as a saving God."),
            S("Significance 2: covenant", text="The Exodus led to Sinai, where the people became Yahweh's covenant people."),
            S("Significance 3: memory and worship", text="The Passover festival remembers it each year, and later prophets appeal to it."),
            S("Significance 4: the promise", text="It began the journey to the land promised to the ancestors."),
            S("Conclusion", text="The Exodus was central because it shaped Israel's identity, covenant, worship and hope."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a plan for an Islam question", intro="Question (written for practice): 'Explain the importance of Ramadan for Muslims.'", steps=[
            S("Define", text="Ramadan is the ninth month of the Islamic calendar, when Muslims fast from dawn to sunset."),
            S("Point 1", text="It is one of the five pillars, so it is a duty and a mark of identity."),
            S("Point 2", text="It is the month when the Quran began to be revealed; Muslims increase recitation and prayer."),
            S("Point 3", text="It builds self-control, empathy for the hungry, charity and forgiveness."),
            S("Point 4", text="The month ends with Id-ul-Fitr, which brings community joy and gifts to the poor."),
            S("Conclusion", text="It matters because it joins worship, discipline and community."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Starting with 'Introduction' and writing nothing", "A heading is not an introduction. Write a first paragraph that defines the term and states your answer. Do the same in the conclusion."),
        TIP("Time. For four essays in the time allowed, give each question the same share, and leave two minutes to read through. The KKEVO practice paper time is our own setting; follow your real paper's instructions. Cross out any rough plan neatly and keep it, since examiners can see that the structure was planned."),
        QUIZ("How many questions does a candidate answer in Paper 2, according to the Board's 2023 report?", [
            "Four, all from one section", "Four, one from each of four sections", "Eighteen", "Six from any section"], 0,
            "The report says that candidates answered four questions chosen from any one section.", hint="Think of sections and questions per section.",
            feedback=["", "There are three sections, and all four answers come from the same one.", "There are 18 questions in the paper, not 18 answers.", "Six is the number of questions in a section."]),
        QUIZ("A question asks about the importance of the Exodus. What does the report say many candidates did?", [
            "Explained its importance in depth", "Narrated the story without emphasising its significance", "Wrote about the wrong religion", "Left it blank"], 1,
            "The report says that many narrated the events without showing why the Exodus was so central.", hint="Think of the faults above.",
            feedback=["That was what the examiners wanted.", "", "The comment concerned the Israelites.", "The report says many attempted it."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Explain the importance of Id-ul-Adha for Muslims.",
           "Plan: define it as the feast of sacrifice (Tabaski) at the end of the Hajj season, remembering Ibrahim's willingness to submit to God; point 1: sacrifice of an animal and sharing meat with family and the poor; point 2: submission to Allah; point 3: unity with pilgrims at Hajj; point 4: charity and community; conclusion: the feast joins obedience, generosity and solidarity. Stay with this feast and do not wander to others."),
        PQ("Assess the role of the Judges in Israel.",
           "Plan: name some judges (for example Deborah, Gideon, Samson, Samuel; check your textbook for the list your course uses); religious role: call the people back to Yahweh; political and military role: protect the tribes from neighbours; the confederacy had no central king; limits: the repeated cycle of disobedience and weakness; conclusion: they kept the tribes together but the lack of unity led to the demand for a king."),
        PQ("Discuss the importance of sacred places in African Traditional Religion.",
           "Plan: define sacred places as sites linked with spirits, ancestors or the Supreme Being, for example hills, caves, lakes, groves and shrines (check your textbook for examples that your course uses); distinguish them from ordinary social places such as the market; explain their importance for worship, sacrifice, healing, community unity and respect for nature; add evaluation: changes caused by modern life and other religions; conclusion."),
        SUM("Paper 2 has 18 essay questions in three sections of six; candidates answer four from one section (Board's 2023 report).",
            "Choose the section where you can answer at least four questions well.",
            "The main fault the report names is narrating the story without explaining significance.",
            "Plan with an introduction, points with evidence, significance, evaluation and a conclusion."),
    ]))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 11. Paper 3
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_EXAM, topic="Paper 3",
    title="Paper 3 strategy: structural questions in one section",
    slug="rsu2-paper-3-strategy",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Paper 3 has 36 structural questions in six sections of six; candidates answer four from one section. This lesson explains the sections, how to answer parts of a structural question, and the faults the Board's report describes.",
    objectives=[
        "State the structure and sections of Paper 3 as the Board's 2023 report describes them",
        "Answer a structural question part by part",
        "Show knowledge of the context of a passage or a teaching",
        "Avoid the faults named in the report, such as retelling or missing the context",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Paper 3 structure", "Structural question", "Context", "Part-by-part answers", "Philosophy of Religion", "Ethics and Morality"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A structural question is like a short staircase: it divides a topic into smaller steps, and each step asks something particular. Instead of one long essay you answer several connected parts, such as 'state', 'explain' and 'discuss'. This makes it easy to see what you do and do not know, so careful preparation of one section gives a real advantage."),
        H("The structure of Paper 3"),
        D("According to the Board's 2023 report, **Paper 3** had **36 structural questions** in **six sections of six questions**. Candidates had to answer **four suitable questions from one section of their choice**. The paper carries about **33%** of the examination, and the report gives the marks for each question as out of 25. The six sections named in 2023 were: the Gospel of Luke; John, the Fourth Gospel; the Early Church as reflected in the Acts of the Apostles and First Corinthians; Philosophy of Religion; Ethics and Morality; and the Historical Development of Islam. Check which sections your school prepares.", title="Paper 3 in the Board's report"),
        T("In the Alternative Track of this course, the lessons on the problem of evil and religious language and on bioethics support the Philosophy of Religion and the Ethics and Morality sections, and the lesson on Islamic theology and jurisprudence supports the Islam section. The lesson on the Sermon on the Mount and on eschatology support the Gospel sections and the New Testament ethics of your course. Check which track your school teaches, since the track you follow decides which sections you prepare."),
        H("How to answer a structural question"),
        KEY("Four habits", "Use them in every part.", cards=[
            ["Read all parts first", "The parts build on each other. Part (a) may give you words for part (c)."],
            ["Match the answer to the command", "State and name need short answers; explain and discuss need reasons and development."],
            ["Match the length to the marks", "A short part needs a short answer. A longer part needs several developed points."],
            ["Label your answers", "Write (a), (b), (c) so that the examiner can find each one."],
        ]),
        H("What the report says went wrong"),
        T("The report's comments, paraphrased and grouped, show where marks were lost in Paper 3."),
        KEY("Typical faults", "Learn the pattern so that you can avoid it.", cards=[
            ["Not placing the answer in context", "On the attitude of Jesus to those he met in Palestine, candidates did not set their answers in the Palestinian setting and wrote instead about the Pharisees. On Paul's teaching about marriage, many wrote about the problems of marriage in general and did not link them to the Corinthian church. On food offered to idols, candidates were expected to show the situation in Corinth."],
            ["Narrating a parable or story", "On the Good Samaritan, many told the story and did not bring out the teaching on love and discipleship. On Lazarus, candidates did not bring out the significance of the sign. On the trial and crucifixion in John, many wrote about the trial and did not link it to the identity of Jesus as the promised Saviour."],
            ["Missing the argument in philosophical questions", "In Philosophy of Religion, candidates needed to define the attributes of God, handle Kant's view, and set out the arguments of Aquinas (the Five Ways); several wrote general opinion instead. In Ethics, questions on utilitarianism, rules and freedom, evil and suffering and falsification needed accurate technical terms."],
            ["Accuracy about history", "In the Islam section, candidates were expected to know the period before Islam, the Night Journey, the compiling of the Quran, the Farewell Sermon, the death of the Prophet and the life of Abu Bakr."],
            ["Missing evidence for authorship or purpose", "On the authorship of Luke, candidates were expected to give points showing that Luke and Acts have the same author, such as shared style, preface and addressee."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: a structural question on Luke", intro="Question (written for practice): (a) Name the person to whom Luke dedicated his Gospel. (b) State two reasons for the view that Luke and Acts have the same author. (c) Explain why this matters for understanding the story of the church.", steps=[
            S("(a) Name", text="Theophilus (Luke 1:3 and Acts 1:1)."),
            S("(b) Two reasons", text="Both works are addressed to Theophilus; Acts begins by referring to a 'former book'. Both share style, vocabulary and themes such as the Holy Spirit and concern for the poor and outsiders."),
            S("(c) Importance", text="Luke and Acts form one story in two volumes, from Jesus' ministry to the spread of the church, so Acts shows what the apostles did because of what Jesus had taught."),
            S("Check", text="Each part has been answered separately and labelled."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a context answer on Paul and marriage", intro="Question (written for practice): Why did Paul write about marriage in 1 Corinthians 7?", steps=[
            S("The setting", text="The Corinthian church lived in a port city with loose sexual morals. Some members thought that sex should be avoided, and others lived immorally."),
            S("The question", text="The Corinthians wrote to Paul asking about marriage and singleness (1 Corinthians 7:1)."),
            S("Paul's answers", text="Marriage is good and partners owe each other love and respect; singleness is a gift; divorce is discouraged; believers should stay with partners who agree to stay."),
            S("The link", text="Each teaching responds to a real situation in Corinth, so it is not a general lecture on marriage."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Writing everything you know", "A structural question gives you a target. If a part asks for two reasons, give two clear reasons, and then move on. Extra material wastes time, and a wrong extra statement may lose marks."),
        TIP("Choose a section in the first minutes, with at least four questions you can answer well. The KKEVO practice settings for time are our own. Use your real paper's instructions."),
        QUIZ("How many questions does a candidate answer in Paper 3, according to the Board's 2023 report?", [
            "Six, one from each section", "Four, from a section of their choice", "Thirty-six", "Two"], 1,
            "The report says that candidates answered four questions from one section of their choice.", hint="There are six sections of six questions.",
            feedback=["That is not what the instructions said.", "", "That is the number of questions set, not the number to answer.", "The number is greater than two."]),
        QUIZ("Why must an answer on Paul's teaching about marriage in 1 Corinthians be linked to Corinth?", [
            "Because Paul wrote only about Corinth", "Because his teaching responds to real problems and questions in that church", "Because examiners dislike general knowledge", "Because Corinth is in Palestine"], 1,
            "Paul answers questions that the Corinthians raised, so the context is part of the answer.", hint="Think of the report's comment on marriage.",
            feedback=["Paul wrote to many places, but this letter has a context.", "", "General knowledge is fine but the focus is context.", "Corinth is in Greece."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("(a) State two attributes of God discussed in the philosophy of religion. (b) Explain one of them.",
           "(a) For example: omnipotence (all-powerful), omniscience (all-knowing), omnipresence (everywhere), eternity, goodness. (b) Omnipotence: God can do all that is logically possible; philosophers discuss whether this includes making contradictions, and whether it conflicts with God's goodness in the problem of evil."),
        PQ("(a) What is meant by utilitarianism? (b) Explain how it would judge an act.",
           "(a) A theory that the right action produces the greatest happiness or the least suffering for the greatest number. (b) It judges by consequences: it counts the happiness and pain caused to all affected, and picks the act with the best total result. Add one criticism: it can neglect the rights of minorities."),
        PQ("(a) What was the 'Farewell Sermon' in the history of Islam? (b) Describe two points of its message.",
           "(a) The sermon the Prophet Muhammad gave at Mount Arafat during his last pilgrimage (the Board's report describes it as on Mount Arafat). (b) For example: kind treatment of women and the vulnerable; reminders of the pillars; that all believers are equal; that Islam is the final religion for Muslims. Check your textbook for the points your course names."),
        SUM("Paper 3 has 36 structural questions in six sections of six; candidates answer four from one section (Board's 2023 report).",
            "Sections in 2023: Luke, John, Acts and 1 Corinthians, Philosophy of Religion, Ethics and Morality, and the Historical Development of Islam.",
            "Answer part by part, match the command word, and place answers in their context.",
            "Avoid retelling, missing the focus and vague opinion where technical terms are needed."),
    ]))
